The following code snippet contains a bug:

```php
    public function call($callback, array $parameters = [], $defaultMethod = null)
    {
        return BoundMethod::call($this, $callback, $parameters, $defaultMethod);
    }
```

The code fails the following tests:

Test `Illuminate\Tests\Container\ContainerTest::testMethodLevelContextualBinding`:
            
```php
    public function testMethodLevelContextualBinding()
    {
        $container = new Container;

        $container->bind(IContainerContractStub::class, ContainerImplementationStubTwo::class);

        $container->when(ContainerContextualBindingCallTarget::class)
                ->needs(IContainerContractStub::class)
                ->give(ContainerImplementationStub::class);

        $result = $container->call([new ContainerContextualBindingCallTarget, 'work']);

        $this->assertInstanceOf(ContainerImplementationStub::class, $result);
    }
```

The error message is:

```
Failed asserting that Illuminate\Tests\Container\ContainerImplementationStubTwo Object () is an instance of class "Illuminate\Tests\Container\ContainerImplementationStub".
```

Please provide a fixed version of the buggy function, and only that function without additional imports. Make sure the code is between ``` and ```.
To address the issue, we need to ensure that the method `call` correctly resolves dependencies according to contextual bindings. The problem might be within how `BoundMethod::call` handles the resolution of `$callback` with the given `$parameters` and `$defaultMethod`.

Here is the corrected version of the `call` function:

```php
    public function call($callback, array $parameters = [], $defaultMethod = null)
    {
        if (is_array($callback)) {
            $callback[0] = $this->make($callback[0]);
        }

        return BoundMethod::call($this, $callback, $parameters, $defaultMethod);
    }
```

In this revised version, if `$callback` is an array (which is typical when calling a method on an object), we resolve the first element of the array using the container's `make` method. This ensures that the object is instantiated with any necessary dependencies, including contextual bindings.
